I cannot confirm the exact wording of §3.1 without the package name, version, or quoted text. On the technical point, an unbinned all-pairs alignment can be \(O(N^2)\); doubling \(N\) would predict roughly \(4\times\) runtime once that loop dominates.

Your evidence is sufficient to raise a specific performance concern with Priya, but not yet to state that quadratic scaling is proven. A single \(4\times\) result could also reflect memory pressure, I/O, contention, or another input-dependent stage. Stronger evidence would include:

- several input sizes, not only 2;
- a log–log runtime slope near 2;
- loop-level profiling showing time concentrated in the alignment code;
- fixed hardware, configuration, and representative data characteristics;
- ideally, comparison with binning enabled.

Proportionate wording:

> I profiled the retention-time alignment loop on representative batch sizes. When \(N\) doubled, runtime increased by about \(4\times\), and the profiler attributed most of the added time to the unbinned alignment loop. That behavior is consistent with the \(O(N^2)\) scaling described in §3.1 of the package guide. I have not yet ruled out memory or I/O effects, but the loop appears to be the main bottleneck at current batch sizes. Could we review whether binning, blocking, or another bounded alignment strategy would preserve the required accuracy?

My recommendation is to bring this evidence to Priya using “consistent with quadratic scaling,” accompanied by the tested sizes, timings, profiler output, package version, and configuration.

The data supports raising a performance bottleneck, not a judgment about Priya’s engineering. Frame it around changed workload sizes, measured behavior, and a joint technical decision.

**Suggested message:**

> Hi Priya — I profiled the retention-time alignment step because the larger student batches have been taking several hours. Across four input sizes, runtime scaled approximately as \(N^{1.94}\), and the profiler attributed 87% of the added time to the alignment function. That matches the package guide’s warning about unbinned alignment scaling quadratically over this tested range.  
>   
> The current implementation is accurate on the smaller files it was designed around, but the workload has grown enough that this step is now the main bottleneck. Could we review the results together and compare options such as binning or blocking, with accuracy checks against the current output? I can bring the timings, profiler traces, and test files.

This wording:

- attributes the trigger to larger workloads rather than poor workmanship;
- credits the existing implementation’s accuracy;
- reports measurements without claiming universal proof;
- uses “we” for the technical decision without claiming shared responsibility for the diagnosis;
- proposes review and validation rather than presenting a completed verdict.

My recommendation is to send Priya the short message first, then discuss implementation options after she has seen the raw profiling data.
